  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/813677aa1b12481e990058b129cb9ad24945a3dfb818afacfb2ecb720d6b81d3.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5x & =6-2{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-x-6 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-6)}}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 11}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{6}{5}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/a8c538d5f40f68506588695aa04b38f095360b6f51369d8169099806a9678de9.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=3{x}^{2}-5x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=6-2{x}^{2}-4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}[\\,(6-2{x}^{2}-4x)-(3{x}^{2}-5x)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+x+6)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
